Now I have a thorough understanding of the entire codebase. Let me produce the comprehensive implementation plan.

---

# Comprehensive Implementation Plan: Samson Tilahun Moges Academic Portfolio Redesign

## Preliminary Audit Findings

**Merge conflict (lines 747–757 of `css/style.css`):** The `main` branch version is correct. It has `avatar-img` (the real `<img>` tag), while the `copilot` branch had `avatar-placeholder` (the old text-initials fallback that no longer exists in the HTML). The correct resolution keeps these four declarations and nothing else:

```
.hero-name { font-size: 1.9rem; }
.avatar-img { width: 120px; height: 120px; }
.btn { padding: 9px 18px; font-size: 0.85rem; }
```

The `about-stats flex-direction: column` and `stat-divider` rules from the `copilot` branch are useful responsive additions but belong in the correct block with `.avatar-img` — they can be re-added as supplemental rules since both sets are actually complementary (the flex-direction column rule was simply missing from main).

**File inventory:**
- `index.html` — 574 lines, all publications inline
- `css/style.css` — 760 lines (759 effective, 1 conflict block)
- `js/main.js` — 85 lines, vanilla JS
- No `favicon.ico`, no `og:image`, no `cv.pdf`
- No `js/` subdirectory scripts other than `main.js`

---

## 1. Recommended Design System

### Color Scheme Decision: Option A — Deep Navy / Teal / Gold

**Recommendation: Option A**, with rationale:

Option C (academicpages white+blue+orange) is already close to the current site. Option B (dark charcoal+cyan) reads as a developer portfolio, not a scientist's. Option A — deep navy base with teal accent and warm gold highlights — is the canonical "credible scientist" palette. It evokes the night sky, aurora phenomena, and the Finnish/Arctic setting of Samson's research. It differentiates from the commodity academic blue while remaining conservative enough for hiring committees at universities.

**Complete Token Set:**

```
Light Mode (default):
--color-bg:             #f7f8fc        (off-white with slight blue cast)
--color-bg-alt:         #eef1f8        (subtle layering)
--color-bg-card:        #ffffff
--color-surface:        rgba(255,255,255,0.72)   (glass cards)

--color-primary:        #0d2b54        (deep navy)
--color-primary-mid:    #1a4480        (mid navy for hover)
--color-primary-light:  #dce8f5
--color-accent:         #0e9e8e        (teal)
--color-accent-light:   #cdf0ec
--color-gold:           #c8952a        (warm gold – used sparingly)
--color-gold-light:     #fdf0d4

--color-text:           #0f1c2e        (near-black navy)
--color-text-muted:     #4a5e78
--color-text-light:     #7a90a8
--color-border:         #d4dce8
--color-border-subtle:  #eaeff6

Dark Mode (data-theme="dark"):
--color-bg:             #08111e
--color-bg-alt:         #0d1a2b
--color-bg-card:        #111f33
--color-surface:        rgba(17,31,51,0.80)
--color-primary:        #4a9edd        (bright sky blue – primary inverts to light in dark)
--color-primary-mid:    #72b8f0
--color-primary-light:  #0d2b54
--color-accent:         #1fcfbc
--color-accent-light:   #0b3d38
--color-gold:           #e8b84b
--color-gold-light:     #2a1f06
--color-text:           #e8eff8
--color-text-muted:     #8aa3bf
--color-text-light:     #5a7a9a
--color-border:         #1e3450
--color-border-subtle:  #152a40
```

Publication quality badges keep their current colors — they are institution-agnostic signals (green Q1, blue Q2) and work well in both modes with minor dark-mode adjustments.

### Typography Pairing

**Replace Inter + Merriweather with:**

- **Display / Name / Section headings:** `Playfair Display` (wght 600, 700) — has academic gravitas, strong letterforms, works at large sizes, used by Nature, Science magazine layouts
- **Body / UI:** `Source Sans 3` (wght 300, 400, 500, 600) — designed by Adobe for scientific/technical reading, wider glyph set than Inter, superior CJK fallback
- **Monospace (DOI links, data labels):** `JetBrains Mono` (wght 400, 500) — cleaner than the default monospace stack for citation counts and year labels

Google Fonts import (single request, `display=swap`):
```
https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Playfair+Display:wght@600;700&family=Source+Sans+3:wght@300;400;500;600&family=JetBrains+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap
```

### Spacing Scale

```
--space-1:  4px
--space-2:  8px
--space-3:  12px
--space-4:  16px
--space-5:  24px
--space-6:  32px
--space-7:  48px
--space-8:  64px
--space-9:  96px
--space-10: 128px
```

Sections use `--space-9` top/bottom. Cards use `--space-5` padding. Container max-width: `1100px`.

### Border Radius

```
--radius-xs:   4px
--radius-sm:   8px
--radius-md:   14px
--radius-lg:   22px
--radius-xl:   32px
--radius-full: 9999px
```

### Shadow Stack

```
--shadow-xs:  0 1px 2px rgba(13,27,84,0.04)
--shadow-sm:  0 2px 8px rgba(13,43,84,0.07), 0 1px 3px rgba(13,43,84,0.05)
--shadow-md:  0 8px 24px rgba(13,43,84,0.10), 0 2px 8px rgba(13,43,84,0.06)
--shadow-lg:  0 20px 60px rgba(13,43,84,0.14), 0 6px 20px rgba(13,43,84,0.08)
--shadow-glow-teal: 0 0 30px rgba(14,158,142,0.22)
--shadow-glow-navy: 0 8px 32px rgba(13,43,84,0.40)
```

---

## 2. New HTML Structure Outline

The single `index.html` will be restructured. Navigation order and `id` attributes:

```
<html data-theme="light">
<head>
  <!-- Meta: charset, viewport, title, description -->
  <!-- Open Graph / Twitter Card meta -->
  <!-- Favicon links -->
  <!-- Critical CSS (inlined <style> block, ~150 lines) -->
  <!-- Font preconnects -->
  <!-- Font stylesheet -->
  <!-- Main stylesheet -->
</head>
<body>

  <!-- ① Scroll progress bar -->
  <div id="scroll-progress" role="progressbar" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <!-- ② Navbar -->
  <nav id="navbar" ...>
    [brand] [nav links: About, News, Research, Publications, CV, Contact] [theme-toggle button]
  </nav>

  <!-- ③ Hero -->
  <section id="hero" ...>
    [canvas#aurora-canvas OR .hero-bg-geo (CSS-only)]
    [.hero-content: avatar | text block]
    [text: pre-title | name | affiliation | tags | links row]
    [links: Google Scholar btn-primary | ResearchGate btn-outline | Download CV btn-outline | University Profile btn-ghost]
    [.scroll-indicator]
  </section>

  <!-- ④ About -->
  <section id="about" ...>
    [.about-metrics row: Citations | h-index | Publications | Years Active — animated counters]
    [.about-grid: bio text col | highlights col]
    [highlights: position card | dual-region card | publications since card]
  </section>

  <!-- ⑤ News (NEW) -->
  <section id="news" ...>
    [.news-grid: 4–6 .news-item cards in a 2-col grid]
    [each card: date badge | icon | headline | brief description]
  </section>

  <!-- ⑥ Research (ENHANCED) -->
  <section id="research" ...>
    [.research-projects: 2-3 featured project cards with methodology callouts]
    [.interests-grid: 4 interest cards (keep existing)]
  </section>

  <!-- ⑦ Timeline/CV (NEW) -->
  <section id="cv" ...>
    [.timeline: vertical line with alternating .timeline-item nodes]
    [items: MSc 2013–2015, Various early positions, PhD start 2019, PhD defense 2026, Postdoc]
  </section>

  <!-- ⑧ Publications (ENHANCED) -->
  <section id="publications" ...>
    [.pub-controls: search input | filter buttons | year-range display]
    [.pub-stats-bar: mini citation chart (canvas element)]
    [.pub-year-group × 7 years: existing card structure retained]
    [.scholar-cta]
  </section>

  <!-- ⑨ Contact -->
  <section id="contact" ...>
    [.contact-grid: 5 contact cards — existing structure, visual refresh only]
  </section>

  <!-- ⑩ Footer -->
  <footer ...>
    [copyright | social links | "built with" attribution]
  </footer>

  <script src="js/main.js"></script>
</body>
```

---

## 3. Section-by-Section Implementation Details

### Section A: Merge Conflict Fix

In `css/style.css` lines 747–758, replace the entire conflict block with the resolved version that merges both branch contributions:

```css
@media (max-width: 480px) {
  .hero-name { font-size: 1.9rem; }
  .avatar-img { width: 120px; height: 120px; }
  .btn { padding: 9px 18px; font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .about-stats { flex-direction: column; gap: 16px; }
  .stat-divider { width: 80%; height: 1px; }
}
```

The `.avatar-placeholder` rule from the copilot branch is discarded (no element with that class exists in HTML). The `about-stats` column layout is a genuine improvement worth keeping from the copilot branch.

### Section B: `<head>` Additions

Add immediately after the `<title>` tag:

```html
<!-- Favicon (SVG inline or external) -->
<link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="favicon.svg">
<link rel="alternate icon" href="favicon.ico">

<!-- Open Graph -->
<meta property="og:title" content="Samson Tilahun Moges – Space Physicist">
<meta property="og:description" content="Postdoctoral researcher at Sodankylä Geophysical Observatory studying ionospheric disturbances, GPS TEC, and EISCAT radar.">
<meta property="og:image" content="https://samson-moges.github.io/og-image.png">
<meta property="og:url" content="https://samson-moges.github.io/">
<meta property="og:type" content="website">
<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">

<!-- Theme color for browser chrome -->
<meta name="theme-color" content="#0d2b54" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)">
<meta name="theme-color" content="#08111e" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)">
```

Update the font import to the new triple-family URL above.

Add a `<style>` block immediately before `</head>` containing critical-path CSS: the `:root` custom properties, `body` base styles, `.navbar` styles, and the `.hero` background gradient. This ensures the page is visually stable before the external CSS loads, eliminating flash of unstyled content (FOUC) on repeat visits.

### Section C: Scroll Progress Indicator

HTML: A `<div id="scroll-progress">` as the very first child of `<body>`.

CSS:
```css
#scroll-progress {
  position: fixed;
  top: 0;
  left: 0;
  height: 3px;
  width: 0%;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--color-accent), var(--color-gold));
  z-index: 1100;
  transition: width 0.1s linear;
  border-radius: 0 var(--radius-full) var(--radius-full) 0;
}
```

JS (added to `main.js`):
```js
const progressBar = document.getElementById('scroll-progress');
window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
  const scrolled = window.scrollY;
  const total = document.documentElement.scrollHeight - window.innerHeight;
  progressBar.style.width = (scrolled / total * 100) + '%';
}, { passive: true });
```

### Section D: Dark/Light Mode Toggle

**In HTML** — Add `data-theme="light"` to `<html>`. Add a toggle button inside the navbar, after the nav-links:

```html
<button class="theme-toggle" id="themeToggle" aria-label="Toggle dark mode">
  <svg class="icon-sun" ...><!-- sun SVG --></svg>
  <svg class="icon-moon" ...><!-- moon SVG --></svg>
</button>
```

**In CSS** — All color custom properties under `:root` define the light mode. Add a `[data-theme="dark"]` block that overrides every `--color-*` token. This means zero conditional styling elsewhere in the stylesheet — any component that uses a CSS variable gets dark mode automatically.

```css
[data-theme="dark"] {
  --color-bg: #08111e;
  --color-bg-alt: #0d1a2b;
  /* ... all dark tokens from design system above ... */
}
```

Navbar dark mode: the `rgba(255,255,255,0.94)` background must become `rgba(8,17,30,0.94)` — this is achieved via a `--color-nav-bg` token.

**In JS:**

```js
const themeToggle = document.getElementById('themeToggle');
const root = document.documentElement;

// Respect system preference as default, override with stored value
const stored = localStorage.getItem('theme');
const prefersDark = window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;
const initial = stored || (prefersDark ? 'dark' : 'light');
root.setAttribute('data-theme', initial);

themeToggle.addEventListener('click', () => {
  const next = root.getAttribute('data-theme') === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark';
  root.setAttribute('data-theme', next);
  localStorage.setItem('theme', next);
});
```

The `data-theme` attribute must be set by an inline script in `<head>` (before CSS loads) to prevent a flash of wrong theme. Add this as the first `<script>` tag, inline, in `<head>`:

```html
<script>
  (function(){
    var t = localStorage.getItem('theme') ||
      (window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches ? 'dark' : 'light');
    document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', t);
  })();
</script>
```

### Section E: Hero Redesign

**Aurora/Geometric Background (CSS-only):**

The hero background uses layered pseudo-elements with radial gradients and a CSS `@keyframes` animation. No canvas or external library needed. Structure:

```
.hero { position: relative; overflow: hidden; }
.hero::before { /* large slow-moving radial aurora blob */ }
.hero::after  { /* second smaller blob, different color */ }
.hero-bg-geo  { /* retained element, now carries CSS-animated mesh lines */ }
```

The aurora effect is achieved with `filter: blur(80px)` on large colored ellipses that slowly scale and shift using `animation: aurora-drift 14s ease-in-out infinite alternate`. This is a proven, GPU-friendly technique used by Stripe, Linear, and Vercel for hero sections.

**CSS for aurora:**
```css
.hero::before {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  width: 700px;
  height: 500px;
  top: -10%;
  left: -15%;
  background: radial-gradient(ellipse, rgba(14,158,142,0.18) 0%, transparent 65%);
  filter: blur(60px);
  animation: aurora-drift 16s ease-in-out infinite alternate;
  pointer-events: none;
}
.hero::after {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  width: 600px;
  height: 400px;
  bottom: -5%;
  right: -10%;
  background: radial-gradient(ellipse, rgba(13,43,84,0.22) 0%, transparent 60%);
  filter: blur(70px);
  animation: aurora-drift 20s ease-in-out infinite alternate-reverse;
  pointer-events: none;
}
@keyframes aurora-drift {
  0%   { transform: translate(0, 0) scale(1); }
  50%  { transform: translate(40px, -30px) scale(1.08); }
  100% { transform: translate(-20px, 20px) scale(0.95); }
}
```

**Hero layout:** Retain flex row (avatar left, text right). Update avatar border to use a `conic-gradient` ring that slowly rotates — creates a subtle "energy field" effect appropriate for a physicist:

```css
